[Music]  by 1000 BCE Spain was sparsely populated  by various small communities in which  people lived by farming fishing and  hurting by then other people in Europe  and the Mediterranean had heard of Spain  and more people began to arrive already  at the dawn of history Spain was a  desirable destination and as we will see  it remains so today in this lecture I  will describe three different groups of  immigrants the Celts the Phoenicians and  the Greeks who all retained their  identity as they moved to Spain they  also left their mark on the increasingly  diverse peninsula some came by sea but  the earliest came overland through the  passes in the Pyrenees let's begin with  the Celts the Oregon the origins of the  Celts are shrouded in mystery Celtic  language is an indo-european tongue  which means that speakers originated  some 12,000 years ago somewhere north of  the Black Sea in the Russian steppes  some of these tribes that can be clearly  identified as Celtic settled in Central  Europe where they spread to many parts  of Europe including Spain we are not  sure of the dates for the movement of  small groups of Celtic tribes from their  homeland but it seems that from about  1400 BCE through 600 BCE small groups of  these Celtic tribes left Central Europe  and periodically crossed the Pyrenees  through the western passes and settled  in Spain at first they spread widely  throughout the peninsula but in most  regions they combined with a local  population however isolated in the  northwest mountains they preserved much  of their culture this region of Spain is  called Galicia there is a region named  Galicia in Central Europe between Poland  and Ukraine as well and that also was an  area of Celtic settlement  there is no consensus on what the word  galicia really means but it always  designates regions of celtic settlement  and that remains true in spain the name  galicia is also related to gaul the  region of modern France that was  inhabited by Celts in Roman times  the Celts contributed a number of items  to Spain's cultural legacy but the Celts  who entered about 800 BCE brought the  most important innovation iron how did I  earn replace bronze in weapons and tools  by about 1200 BCE increased warfare  particularly at sea in the eastern  Mediterranean interrupted the usual  trade routes making tin scarce so  metalsmiths could not make bronze the  alloy of tin and copper smelters in Asia  Minor began to experiment with iron an  abundant mineral in that region unforced  I is not much stronger than copper and  no match for bronze but when it's  repeatedly heated in a hot charcoal  furnace carbon molecules combine with  the iron to form a reliable metal known  as carbon steel which is strong enough  to slice through bronze armed with iron  weapons and knowledge of the new  technology Kelsey came into Spain the  iron age had dawned and it dominated the  world until the late 19th century when  metal Smith's developed new ways to make  iron into steel without the carbon  method this is called Bessemer steel the  strong warlike Celts settled in walled  hilltop towns called castra and their  ruins still docked the mountain heights  in galicia within the walls  they lived in round stone huts and  guarded their most precious commodity  herds of great long horned cattle the  land of galicia is beautiful it rains a  lot  so the rolling hills and mountains are  green with forests of oak myrtle and  pine and the cattle can graze in valleys  with meadows of low weeds and thorny  bushes  the folding topography isolates each  Valley increasing the tendency of each  village to think of itself as  independent this geographic isolation  was true even if coastal fishing  villages because the hills do not taper  off as they reach the shore but they  continue to dominate the land until they  plunge into deep fjords called Ria's  coastal villages cling to the hills  facing the sea you think that this lush  green landscape would produce abundance  but it doesn't the earth is rocky and  the excessive rain permits a harvest  only once a year unlike the two or three  crops per year yielded by sunny  irrigated lands further south  Strabo a greek geographer and 18c ii  wrote one of the earliest descriptions  of this region capturing the difficulty  of life here in the northwest  he claimed northern iberia in addition  to its ruggedness not only is extremely  cold but it lies next to the ocean  consequently it's an exceedingly  wretched place to live in nevertheless  the Celts in this region thrived they  built their fortified mountain towns out  of stone and men hunted and fished and  worked iron the women did the  agricultural work planting wheat and  supplementing meager yields by gathering  acorns in the forests a 7th century  Spanish Bishop fructose of Braga wrote  with some accuracy that Galicia requires  more work on the soil than any other  land the Celts of Spain who came to be  called Celt iberian z' worships nature  just as celts elsewhere had done oak  trees springs mountains and other  natural forms were venerated as people  brought items to sacrifice for the many  gods and goddesses that inhabited the  natural world as we will see christian  leaders had a hard time persuading these  independent celts to give up their  traditional rituals that had stood them  well in these rugged ills  even today this Northwest province of  Galicia preserves traces of its Celtic  roots the regional musical instrument is  the guitar or bagpipes and when I listen  to them played it made me think I was in  Scotland the stone houses in the green  countryside feel like Ireland and many  of the words in the Galician dialect  called Gahagan have Celtic roots this  northwest province of Spain joins the  Basque as another of the unique regions  that was established in the early  settlement periods like many other  scholars I used to think that the  Spanish celts resemblance to the celts  in the british isles was due to their  common homeland however recent DNA  studies have shown that Spain served as  a crossroad for Celts moving in the  Bronze Age it turns out that all the  Celts in the British Isles originally  came from Celts and Spain who had  crossed the Bay of Biscay during the  Bronze Age this stunning find means that  during the Bronze Age hardy Celts in  Spain developed boats that could handle  the rough Atlantic waters there's a  magnificent Bronze Age boat that was  found in Dover England and may be this  kind of boat that let the celts from  spain settle the northern islands this  boat is made of oak planks lashed  together with ropes of fibers from the  yew tree the Farabee boats which are  centuries older than the dover boat use  a similar construction reproductions  show a straight bow to ward off the  waves while incredibly sturdy oarsmen  road there must have been boats  something like this that allowed the  spanish celts  to move beyond their well-established  settlements in galicia and spread north  by 1100 BCE long after the celts and  figured out how to build a boat that  they could row into the atlantic there  had been a long tradition of ships  crossing the Mediterranean  the next important immigrants into Spain  didn't cross the  pyrénées like the Celts had they crossed  the Mediterranean among the most  successful of these maritime traders  were the Phoenicians whose culture was  based on the shores of the eastern  Mediterranean in the cities of Sidon  tire and byblos the Phoenicians were  known throughout the ancient world for  their maritime skills and trading  abilities one ancient roman writer  called the masters of the fleet that  controlled the sea in Homer's Odyssey  they were called the men famed for their  ships in the Bible the prophet Ezekiel  referred to the Phoenicians as the  merchant to the peoples of many  coastlines their trade took them  throughout the Mediterranean and they  established a presence in Spain ancient  sailors in the Mediterranean were caught  us they did not sail out of the sight of  land which was made easier by the  mountains that surround the sea however  they could not sail at night so each  night they beached their ships to sleep  sailing out again the next morning to  guarantee safe harbours Phoenician  traders established merchant colonies  all along the north coast of Africa by  some estimates there was a colony about  every 30 miles the most important colony  was carthage in north africa which was  founded about 800 BCE and as we will see  carthage will play an important role in  spain the Phoenicians were the first  peoples to link the eastern in the  western Mediterranean and a sail through  the treacherous Strait of Gibraltar into  the Atlantic from there the traders went  north as far as the British Isles and  even south down the coast of Africa the  most important colony they established  in the western Mediterranean was Cadiz  Spain probably the oldest continuously  inhabited city in Europe when the  Phoenicians arrived in Spain around 1100  BCE they didn't bring weapons they  arrived with trade goods and were  welcomed as they came into the Atlantic  they found Cadiz which was the perfect  port for ships  ancient and modern the city lies in a  Cove with a spit of land protecting it  from the Atlantic storms there's a  beautiful beach where ancient sailors  could pull up their ships for the night  today travelers can see small fishing  boats floating were the ancient traders  landed archaeological excavations in  Cadiz show the Phoenicians name the city  Ghadeer which means stronghold  indicating how defensible the city is  they built a large temple on the south  end dedicated to their God Mel carte  with giant columns that could be seen  from ships approaching the strait when  the Phoenicians settled in the area  around Cadiz they found some small wild  grapes but they had brought along their  own grape plants they started some small  vineyards and began to make wine that  wonderful taste of the eastern  Mediterranean subsequent invaders would  build on and improve this beginning  effort until today when Spanish wines  are world-famous  but the real Phoenician contribution  came with their skill in engineering the  port of Cadiz may have been a fine for  Phoenician traders but the settlers  exploited an even greater find the  silver mines of Rio Tinto about 125  miles northwest from Cadiz ancient  miners since the Bronze Age had worked  these mines excavating copper and silver  but those are only two of the metals  that this region would yield Rio Tinto  was one of the largest mining complexes  in the ancient world and its origins are  impossible to trace local folklore  claims that Rio Tinto was the site of  the biblical King Solomon's Mines and  the fame of the mines and the ancient  world makes this possible visitors today  can see the almost moonscape that was  left in the wake of millennia of mining  in fact NASA has used Rio Tinto for its  robots to drill as practice for the  geological excavations on Mars  the Phoenicians brought new tech  ecology to the minds and silver  extractions spiked during their stay the  Phoenicians were highly motivated to  extract silver from the mines because  they owed the warlike Assyrians their  neighbors back in the eastern  Mediterranean an annual tribute of large  quantities of silver as we can see even  three thousand years ago Spain remained  in the crossroads of trade from the  Atlantic coast all the way to the  mesopotamian Valley where Assyrians grew  rich on Spanish silver what kind of  improvements did the Phoenician  engineers bring to the mines they add  added rectangular plane nozzles to the  giant leather bellows which pumped air  into the smelting furnaces permitting  bigger ovens higher temperatures more  successful melting and the extraction of  larger amounts of metal the boom and  mining led more Phoenician settlers into  the region and more interaction between  Spain and the eastern Mediterranean in  addition to mining improvements the  Phoenicians greatest contribution to  Western culture was their remarkable  alphabet they improved on older systems  like that of the Egyptians by creating a  simple script based on a purely phonetic  alphabet of only 22 letters this  simplified system spread rapidly  including into Spain and allowed more  people to learn to read and write thanks  to the Phoenicians writing no longer  required years of study excavations in  Spain have yielded interesting  Carthaginian sculptures and my favorite  is a sculpture of a woman with her hands  extended she might be the Carthaginian  goddess tenet she wears a necklace made  of terracotta beads and there's enough  color left in the beads to see that  they're the same blue charms against the  evil eye that are sold all across the  Mediterranean today these fake blue eyes  are supposed to draw the evil eye of  jealousy and bad luck that flows through  the universe the evil eye is drawn to  the charm leaving the wearer free from  harm  all over the Mediterranean parents today  continue to hang these charms by their  children's beds to keep them safe I have  my own on a backpack as I travel and  it's fun to see how old these charms are  our knowledge of the Phoenicians was  greatly enhanced in 2007 when a wreck  was excavated about 20 miles northeast  of cafe Hana Spain here in the sea a  rock formation called the bajo de la  campaña rises suddenly from a depth of  nearly 60 feet to within three feet of  the surface this rock has claimed many  vessels though both ancient and modern  including a Phoenician merchant ship  from the seventh century BCE the Spanish  Ministry of Education Culture and sport  conducted more than 4,000 dives at the  wreck between 2007 and 2011 and the  resulting finds have shaped our  understanding of the ancient Phoenicians  this ship had been sailing along the  Iberian coast and likely stopped at  various ports along the way the crew  offloaded goods and acquired new cargo  and one of the surprising finds of the  excavation was the variety of goods in  trade in exchange for silver and other  metals from Rio Tinto Phoenicians made  luxury products from the East or in  demand huge lumps of amber from the  Baltic Sea in the far north joined  ostrich shells bronze furniture and even  carved ivory from Africa researchers  find this single ship a microcosm of the  vast trade networks of the Phoenicians  that joined the Baltic in the north to  sub-saharan Africa and the Far Eastern  Mediterranean to the Atlantic Ocean part  of the fuel for this huge exchange  Network came from the mines of Spain and  it's excellent ports global trade is not  a uniquely modern phenomenon maritime  treasure hunters will immediately notice  what's missing from this excavation  coins  the Phoenicians like all ancient peoples  used a barter system of trade and the  variety of goods on the ship reflects  that the Greek geographer strabo tells  of Phoenician trading practices on the  coast of Africa the Phoenician ship  would leave a pile of goods on shore  then robat back out to anchor local  tribes would leave what was in their  opinion a pile of comparable value on  the shore  the Phoenicians came ashore if the local  offering was acceptable  they sailed away if not they would  remove a portion of their goods until  the deal was mutually satisfactory once  the Phoenician ship left the deal was  done such practices required mutual  trust and complex understandings of what  the goods might be worth trade becomes  simpler with the invention of coinage  that will come with the next traders and  colonizers of Spain the Greeks some time  around 800 BCE Greeks began to leave  their rocky peninsula and established  colonies around the Black Sea and the  northern shores of the Mediterranean at  first they settled on numerous islands  of the Aegean and the coast of Asia  Minor modern Turkey then they went  farther why did they travel like the  Phoenicians Greeks sailors needed ports  to put in at overnight but the Greeks  had a more conscious idea to establish  small Greek like city-states as they  went  sometimes aristocrats and Greek cities  use the founding of new colonies as a  way to diffuse social unrest by sending  the dissatisfied elsewhere an  overpopulated Greek cities sent  colonists out to reduce the pressure on  their own cities however Greek colonies  were very different from modern colonial  efforts because Greeks in the new  settlements were independent from the  mother city the ties to the original  cities were ones of emotion and culture  not colonial control however where Greek  settled they try  formed the land into something  resembling home they imported their gods  their religious rites and transplanted  their favorite foods these  characteristics helped make a Greek  imprint on the lands they settled as  Greek colonists moved west  they settled southern Italy Sicily and  southern France the first recorded visit  of Greeks in Spain came in about 650 BCE  when a ship on its way to Egypt was  blown off-course and landed in southern  Spain the Greek captain and his sailors  were well received by the local king  whose name was arg Antonius man of the  Silver Mountain they exchanged gifts and  the Greek ship returned with a cargo of  silver the likes of which had never  before been seen in Greece so the Greeks  knew of the silver in the south and the  rich Rio Tinto mines but they were never  in a position to exploit those riches  the Phoenicians were too strong in that  area the Greek colonists were content to  find a port that would help their  growing trade along the northern coast  of the Mediterranean they found their  perfect port in the north of Spain the  largest Greek colony in Spain was ambu  Dias on the coast near the Pyrenees  north of barthelona it was founded in  575 BCE and the Greeks named Empoleon  which means trading place it was a  perfect location for trade since it  provided a stopping place between the  great French port of Marseilles and  southern Spain in 535 lured Greeks  arrived to escape the Persian Wars in  the East and the city boomed the Greeks  negotiated trading and commercial  privileges with the Iberian locals and  the city prospered for centuries modern  excavations of the area show the early  Greek city by the sea with its standard  acropolis the high point of the city it  was graced with a great temple to  artemis fines have included beautifully  decorated Greek pottery as fine as that  found in  Nathan's excavations at the cemetery  show that Greeks lived side by side with  the native Iberian 's but each kept  their own cultural traditions Greeks  buried their dead y Liberians cremated  theirs yet both were buried in the same  area the Greeks influenced in Spain  extends far beyond the ruins of their  cities their innovations brought from  the East changed the land and the life  of the peninsula let's start with money  the Phoenicians had perfected the barter  system but the Greeks brought coins  coins seemed to have been invented by  the Lydians in Asia Minor in the 7th  century BCE by minting coins with  precise identifiable values Kings could  greatly facilitate trade and the kings  of Lydia were said to have grown  fabulously rich after their invention  others quickly adopted the practice  including the Greeks the Lydians used  white gold for their coins but the  Greeks used silver which allowed them to  make more coins the Greeks  sometimes used images of their gods or  emblems of civic pride like the famous  coin of Athena with the Athenian owl  sometimes they play secular images on  them illustrating their exports like  grapes or fish from the 7th century on  coins offer archeologists and historians  clear evidence for the movements of  people and trade goods but coins also  offer insight into what people valued as  they struck images on what they had come  to value more than almost anything cash  in Spain you can see the greatest Greek  contributions to the land without even  realizing it the acres of olive trees  that spread from the south on into the  plains were not native olive trees were  first domesticated between 8,000 and  6,000 years ago somewhere between Turkey  and Syria but this magnificent fruit  spread rapidly Athenians knew their  wealth and health depended on olive  trees that provided food and oil and  they claimed  accurately that olives first grew in  Athens and were a gift given to them by  Athena they even put images of olive  trees on some of their coins both Greek  and Phoenician colonists brought small  olive trees to plant in their new  colonies to be sure they had this staple  of their diet and life these plantings  showed a long-term commitment because it  could take olive trees between 3 and 12  years to bear fruit depending on the  kind of olive but the trees live for a  long time some today are 2,000 years old  the Romans continued this movement  bringing even more olives into Spain  when I stand in these groves that extend  as far as I can see and smell the sweet  olive fragrance on the dry air I can't  imagine this land without the tree that  came from the far eastern Mediterranean  the Greek influence on the peninsula far  exceeded their population in fact in  terms of sheer numbers  the Phoenicians exerted a much greater  influence however the Greeks had  something else historians and writers  who shaped the history of the peninsula  for subsequent travelers for example it  was the Greek historian Herodotus in the  5th century BCE who wrote about the  fabulous silver mines in the south as we  will see romans read this and came with  their soldiers and their engineers Greek  geographers were the first to name the  peninsula Iberia Herodotus wrote that it  was the Phoenicians it was the  Phoenicians who made the Greeks  acquainted with Iberia the name entered  the vocabulary and has continued today  since we refer to the Iberian Peninsula  where did the word come from probably  from the great Ebro River in the north  near the Greek colonies this is the  second longest river on the peninsula  after the Tagus but it's significant as  it flows into the Mediterranean  providing access to the interior of the  peninsula once the Greeks had named the  peninsula  they named the inhabitants calling the  collection of tribes that had lived  there since the Neolithic period  Liberians and once they encountered the  Celts they became known as Kel Tiberius  these names have continued in term odder  nieuw such naming the peninsula was  influential enough but Greek writers  incorporated Iberia into their mythology  significantly through the myth of  Hercules Hercules with an illegitimate  son of Zeus and the gods wife Hera hated  the young Hercules who was a paragon of  strength courage ingenuity and sexual  prowess indeed he was the perfect ideal  of masculinity Hera drove him mad  and he killed his own children to  expiate this crime he had to perform 12  labors that sent him all around the  Mediterranean the 10th labor took him to  Spain for his tenth labor Hercules had  to go to Cadiz the Atlantic port founded  by the Phoenicians there he was supposed  to slay Darion a monster with three  bodies set on two legs and steal his  cattle according to the myth to arrive  there Hercules used his great strength  to smash open the strait that allowed  the waters of the Mediterranean to flow  into the Atlantic the hero of course  successfully killed the monster and  claimed the cattle because of this myth  sailors in the ancient world called this  straight leading from the Mediterranean  to the Atlantic The Pillars of Hercules  in fact there were actual columns on the  rocks that the Phoenicians built to  honor their God milk cart but these were  never known as the pillars of Mel cart  this is a perfect example of the  influence of the Greeks they didn't  control the peninsula or the Strait  but they did control the history the  Iberian Peninsula is recorded history  begins with the Greeks but soon a  greater power than both the Greeks and  the Phoenicians rose in the West to  shape Iberia's future Rome was on the  move

